#version 450
//! `x < 0 ? alpha * x : x`, as a standalone pass. 16 uses in Piper's HiFi-GAN vocoder.
//!
//! # Why this is an op and not an activation
//!
//! Every other activation in this runtime fuses into the layer that produced its input, so
//! there is no standalone pass at all — see `common.glsl`'s `activate`. HiFi-GAN's
//! LeakyReLUs cannot, for two reasons, and both had to be read out of the graph rather than
//! assumed:
//!
//! * They are **pre**-activations. A ResBlock is `h = h + conv(lrelu(h))`, so the LeakyReLU
//!   sits in front of its convolution, not behind one.
//! * The tensor it reads is *also* the residual's other operand. Folding it into the `Add`
//!   that produced `h` would consume `h`, and the skip connection needs it unchanged.
//!
//! # Alpha travels
//!
//! In `param0_bits`, because this export uses **two** different slopes: 0.1 for the fifteen
//! inside the upsampling stages and 0.01 for the one in front of `conv_post`. A hardcoded
//! 0.1 would be wrong for the last layer before the waveform, which is exactly the kind of
//! error that survives every shape check and comes out as a quieter, duller sound.
//!
//! Distinct from `ACT_PRELU`, which is the same function with a *learned* slope per channel
//! read from the weights buffer. Here the slope is one constant for the whole tensor and is
//! not in the file at all.

#include "common.glsl"

void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    float value = float(arena[p.in0 + index]);
    float alpha = uintBitsToFloat(p.param0_bits);
    // The branch is on the sign of a loaded value, so it diverges within a wave. A `mix`
    // on the comparison would too, and this reads as the definition does.
    arena[p.out_at + index] = float16_t(value < 0.0 ? value * alpha : value);
}
